","The maximum amount of oxygen that the air can determine between the atmospheric pressure and the pressure in the atmospheric atmosphere is known as the pressure-pressure difference.

The pressure-pressure difference is calculated as:

\[
\Delta P = P_{\text{atm}} - P_{\text{atm}} = 2100 \text{ m}
\]

where \( P_{\text{atm}} \) is the atmospheric pressure and \( P_{\text{atm}} \) is the atmospheric pressure.

The pressure-pressure difference is given in Pascals (Pa) or Pascals-meter (Pa·m).

The pressure-pressure difference is a measure of the pressure difference required to maintain a pressure difference of 2100 m.

The pressure-pressure difference is given by:

\[
\Delta P = \rho g h
\]

where \( \rho \) is the density of the air, \( g \) is the acceleration due to gravity, and \( h \) is the height.

The density of the air is given by:

\[
\rho = \frac{m}{V} = \frac{m}{2100}
\]

where \( m \) is the mass of the air and \( V \) is the volume of the air.

The acceleration due to gravity is given by:

\[
g = 9.81 \text{ m/s}^2
\]

The pressure-pressure difference is given by:

\[
\Delta P = \frac{m}{2100} \times 9.81 \text{ m/s}^2 \times h
\]

The pressure-pressure difference is given in Pascals (Pa) or Pascals-meter (Pa·m).

