<USER_REQUEST>
A particle is projected upwards from the ground with an initial velocity of \( 20 \, \text{m/s} \). Assuming the acceleration due to gravity is \( 9.8 \, \text{m/s}^2 \) downward and neglecting air resistance, find the maximum height reached by the particle.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To find the maximum height, we use the kinematic equation:

\[ v^2 = u^2 + 2as \]

where:
- \( v = 0 \, \text{m/s} \) (velocity at maximum height)
- \( u = 20 \, \text{m/s} \) (initial velocity)
- \( a = -9.8 \, \text{m/s}^2 \) (acceleration due to gravity)
- \( s = 0 \, \text{m} \) (displacement, which is the height in this case)

Substituting the values:

\[ 0 = (20)^2 + 2(-9.8)s \]
\[ 0 = 400 - 19.6s \]
\[ 19.6s = 400 \]
\[ s = \frac{400}{19.6} \]
\[ s \approx 20.408 \, \text{m} \]

Thus, the maximum height is \(\boxed{20.41 \, \text{m}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

