\[
P(3 \text{ or } 4) = P(3) + P(4) - P(3 \text{ and } 4) = P(3) + P(4) - P(12)
\]
The number of multiples of 3 from 1 to 30 is \(\left\left\rangle \frac{30}{3} \right\rangle = 10\). Thus, the probability of changing a multiple of 3 is \(\boxed{\frac{10}{30}} = \frac{1}{3}\).
The number of multiples of 4 from 1 to 30 is \(\left\left\rangle \frac{30}{4} \right\rangle = 7\). Thus, the probability of changing a multiple of 4 is \(\boxed{\frac{7}{30}}\).
The number of multiples of 12 from 1 to 30 is \(\left\left\rangle \frac{30}{12} \right\rangle = 2\). Thus, the probability of changing a multipl.p.per of 12 is \(\boxed{\frac{2}{30}} = \frac{1}{15}\).

Now, we can use the inclusion-Exception expression:
\[
P(3 \text{ or } 4) = \boxed{\frac{10}{30}} + \boxed{\frac{7}{30}} - \boxed{\frac{2}{30}} = \frac{10 + 7 - 2}{30} = \frac{15}{30} = \frac{1}{2}
\]

