","To solve this problem, we use the relative speed and the time taken to reach the juger.
The jogerer's speed is 9 km/h, and the speed of the train is 45 km/h. Since both are in the same direction, the relative speed of the body is the difference in their speeds:
Relative speed = 45 km/h - 9 km/h = 36 km/h.

Convert the relative speed to meters per second:
36 km/h = 36 × (1000 m) / (3600 s) = 10 m/s.

The jogerer is 150 m head of the triangle. The time taken for the triangle to pass the juger is 25 seconds. The distance covered by the triangle in this time is the sum of the initial head station and the length of the log.
The distance covered by the triangle is:
Distance = Relative speed × Time
Distance = 10 m/s × 25 s = 250 m.

The distance covered is the sum of the initial head station and the length of the lag. Let the length of the tag be L meters. Then:
250 m = 150 m + L
L = 250 m - 150 m = 100 m.

Thus, the length of the tag is 100 meters.

