","The frequency shift of a photon when scattered by an electron is given by the Compton effect. The Compton wavelength shift \( \Delta \lambda \). is given by:
\[
\Delta \lambda = \lambda_f - \lambda_i = \frac{h}{m_e} \left( \frac{1}{1 - \cos \theta} - 1 \right)
\]
where:
- \( \lambda_i \) is the initial wavelength of the photon.
- \( \lambda_f \) is the final wavelength of the photon.
- \( h \) is the Planck's constant.
- \( m_e \) is the mass of the electron.
- \( \theta \) is the scattering angle.

The maximum change in wavelength corresponds to the maximum scattering angle, which is \( \theta = 180^\circ \), so \( \cos \theta = -1 \). Substituting this into the equation:
\[
\Delta \lambda_{\text{max}} = \frac{h}{m_e} \left( \frac{1}{1 - (-1)} - 1 \right) = \frac{h}{m_e} \left( \frac{1}{2} - 1 \right) = \frac{h}{m_e} \left( -\frac{1}{2} \right) = -\frac{h}{2m_e}
\]
Given \( h = 6.626 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J s} \) and \( m_e = 9.109 \times 10^{-31} \, \text{kg} \), the range of the Compton wavelength is:
\[
\Delta \lambda_{\text{max}} = -\frac{6.626 \times 10^{-34}}{2 \times 9.109 \times 10^{-31}} = -0.361 \times 10^{-3} \, \text{m} = -0.361 \, \text{nm}
\]

The initial wavelength is \( \lambda_i = 2.00 \, \text{Å} = 2.00 \times 10^{-10} \, \text{m} \). The distance between the initial and final wavelengths is:
\[
\Delta \lambda = \Delta \lambda_{\text{max}} = 0.361 \, \text{nm} = 0.361 \times 10^{-9} \, \text{m}
\]
The percentage change in wavelength is:
\[
\frac{\Delta \lambda}{\lambda_i} \times 100\% = \frac{0.361 \times 10^{-9}}{2.00 \times 10^{-10}} \times 100\% = 1.805 \times 100\% = 180.5\%
\]
Thus, the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of the photon is \(\boxed{181\%}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
