","To calculate the work done by the gas during the isothermal expansion, we can use the formula for work done in an isothermal process:

\[
W = -NRT \ln \left( \frac{V_2}{V_1} \right)
\]

where:
- \( W = \text{work done} \)
- \( N = \text{number of moles} \)
- \( R = 8.314 \text{ J/mol·K} = 0.0821 \text{ L·atm/mol·K} \) (using L-atm for work)
- \( T = \text{temperature} \)
- \( V_1 = \text{initial volume} \)
- \( V_2 = \text{final volume} \)

Given:
- \( N = 2 \text{ moles} \)
- \( P_1 = 2 \text{ atm} \)
- \( V_1 = 4 \text{ L} \)
- \( V_2 = 8 \text{ L} \)

Since the process is isothermal, the temperature \( T \) is constant. We can use the ideal gas law to find the temperature:

\[
PV = nRT \implies T = \frac{PV}{nR}
\]

Using \( P_1 = 2 \text{ atm} \), \( V_1 = 4 \text{ L} \), \( n = 2 \text{ moles} \), and \( R = 0.0821 \text{ L·atm/mol·K} \):

\[
T = \frac{(2 \text{ atm})(4 \text{ L})}{2 \text{ mol} \times 0.0821 \text{ L·atm/mol·K}} = \frac{8}{0.1642} \text{ K} \approx 48.78 \text{ K}
\]

However, the temperature is not required for calculating the work in this isothermal expansion, as the gas law is derived from the work expression. Substituting the given values into the work formula:

\[
W = -NRT \ln \left( \frac{V_2}{V_1} \right) = -2 \times 0.0821 \times 48.78 \times \ln \left( \frac{8}{4} \right)
\]
\[
W = -2 \times 0.0821 \times 48.78 \times \ln(2) = -0.811 \times 48.78 \times 0.693 \approx -27.5 \text{ L·atm}
\]

Converting to joules:
\[
1 \text{ L atm} = 101.325 \text{ J}
\]
\[
W = -27.5 \times 101.325 \text{ J} \approx -2786 \text{ J}
\]

Thus, the work done by the gas during the isothermal expansion is \(\boxed{-27.5 \text{ L·atm}\) or \(\boxed{-2786 \text{ J}}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
