","(a) The force on the proton is given by \( F = qE \), where \( q = 1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C} \) and \( E = 500 \, \text{N/C} \). The force is:
\[
F = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C}) = 8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}
\]
Using Newton's second law, \( F = ma \), we solve for the acceleration \( a \):
\[
a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} \approx 4.79 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2
\]
The magnitude of the acceleration is \( 4.79 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2 \).

(b) The problem gives the initial velocity \( v_0 = 0 \, \text{m/s} \), the final velocity \( v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s} \), and the acceleration \( a = 4.79 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2 \). We can use the equation:
\[
v = v_0 + at
\]
Solving for \( t \):
\[
t = \frac{v - v_0}{a} = \frac{35,000 - 0}{4.79 \times 10^{10}} = \frac{3.5 \times 10^4}{4.79 \times 10^{10}} \approx 7.32 \times 10^{-7} \, \text{s}
\]
The time it takes to reach \( 35,000 \, \text{m/s} \) is \( 7.32 \times 10^{-7} \, \text{s} \).

